# =============================================================================
# Trivy misconfiguration ignores
# =============================================================================
# Run with:  trivy config --skip-dirs '**/.terraform' .
#
# Same rule as .checkov.yml: every entry is a DECISION with a written
# justification in docs/future-extensions.md under "Scanner findings
# deliberately left open". Add the justification in the same commit.
# =============================================================================

# AWS-0040 (CRITICAL) -- EKS cluster public access enabled.
# Enabled but CIDR-restricted; modules/eks REJECTS 0.0.0.0/0 via variable
# validation, and endpoint_private_access is always on. Disabling it means
# GitHub-hosted runners cannot reach the Kubernetes API and break-glass access
# needs a bastion or VPN. docs/future-extensions.md.
AWS-0040

# AWS-0104 (CRITICAL) -- unrestricted egress to 0.0.0.0/0.
# Node and control-plane security groups. Nodes need egress for image pulls,
# the EKS API, add-on registries and any outbound the app makes. They sit in
# private subnets behind a NAT Gateway with NO inbound path, and IMDS is
# unreachable from pods (hop limit 1). Pinning egress CIDRs for AWS service
# endpoints is not maintainable.
AWS-0104

# AWS-0010 (MEDIUM) -- CloudFront access logging not configured.
# False positive. Logging uses standard logging v2 via
# aws_cloudwatch_log_delivery{,_source,_destination}, which trivy does not
# recognise. The legacy logging_config block is deliberately unused because it
# requires bucket ACLs. See modules/cloudfront-waf/logging.tf.
AWS-0010

# AWS-0017 (LOW) -- CloudWatch log groups not encrypted with a CMK.
# The WAF log groups. Encrypting them requires granting
# logs.<region>.amazonaws.com use of a CMK that also protects the app token --
# widening a key well beyond what the log content warrants. Both sensitive
# headers (authorization, x-echo-pong-origin-verify) are redacted in the
# logging configuration. See modules/cloudfront-waf/waf-logging.tf.
AWS-0017

# AWS-0132 (HIGH) -- S3 encryption should use a CMK.
# The CloudFront access-log bucket and the state ACCESS-LOG bucket use SSE-S3.
# S3 server access logging cannot write to a CMK-encrypted bucket unless the
# delivery principal holds kms:GenerateDataKey, a grant broader than the log
# data warrants. The STATE bucket itself DOES use a CMK (bootstrap/main.tf).
AWS-0132

# AWS-0089 (LOW) -- S3 bucket logging not enabled.
# The log buckets themselves. Logging a log bucket produces a recursive stream
# with no incident value. The state bucket, which is the sensitive one, has
# access logging to a separate bucket.
AWS-0089

# AWS-0342 (MEDIUM) -- IAM policy allows iam:PassRole.
# echo-pong-gh-tf-apply. Deliberate and conditioned: iam:PassedToService must
# be one of eks, ec2, pods.eks, vpc-flow-logs, delivery.logs. Terraform cannot
# create an EKS cluster or a node group without it.
AWS-0342
